15/9/95 Conversation with Recep Çoban (Fazlı Çoban’s son) -YH
Land is limited, so we have to find ways to produce more from the same amount, as our needs are more and risk of bad harvests is also high..(due to disease, hotter seasons, inadequate rain). Technology can intensify so much; but without favorable climatic conditions, and  growth season we cannot profit.
About herding - We have 1/3 less sheep & goats than 1960’s. Reasons are:
less grazing land, expensive feed, not high enough price of Merinos wool...
Even though we breed more Merinos, its wool is cheaper than Karaman; but Merinos lambs are heavier and its meat is more popular..
So, we should breed Merinos for meat, Karaman for wool....and find markets for Tiftik.

Mixed farming - Labor is critical. If members of family are cooperative, this is preferable, especially if they are jointly owners of land. Collective labor in production and processing..all efforts are put collectively; even though they hire labor for herding and farming, the core group of 6-8 people provide for a very good livelihood, with surplus. Labor is just as critical as water. 
Those who are just borderline are small families, or those who have separated from each other, and therefore not cooperating. Dogan family is one of the 5 most prosperous in the village.


















Avsar (AR) is ca. 7 km. north of SÖ, up a steep hill which we did on a tractor. It is a mountain village, inhabited by Bosnak immigrants who practice dry farming and animal husbandry.  It is a miracle that they can even farm this rocky, upland terrain. 
Hakkı Gençdogan and his mother- Village was established over 100 yrs. ago.
NOW, there are 9 HH living here. Two HH travel between Polatlı and Avsar during year. His father was born on the way in Amasya, coming from Yugoslavia. (He would have been 100 now)..They came from “Eskipazar” in Yugoslavia.
The mother says there were just a few HH when they came.. Biggest time was 25 HH. Prior to that it was a big greek village....She has lived here about 60 years...Crops - wheat, barley, corn, oats, bitter vetch (still grown)...Used oxen..it was dry farming (still dry farming).....Little oats because few herds...
Same farming except for tractor; farmed land is limited - cannot expand it; it is 2,000 d. farmed land..One year we plant sunflower, chickpea, lentil, melon on half of it...Next year, we plant wheat and barley on melon fields. We don’t have NADAS. It is all dry farming. How much does it rain? IT IS STILL GOOD RAIN; still we do not have shortages. Used to be more rain and snow; did not use fertilizer. But FERTILIZER is necessary now, to make up for lack of rain and lack of nutrition of the soil.
How was it when you first came? Same as now. Grazing land is ca. 2-3,000 d.
Did you have more herds before? Yes, much more. Now, only 1 HH hAS FLOCKS. It used to be the “YAYLA” of SÖ village..
{Note: Çekirdeksiz was the Yayla of YH, before it was settled}.

There are 40-50 cows, ca. 500-600 sheep and goats. Mainly Merinos. What do you do with the milk? We only process it for our own consumption..We can’t market it, because there is not enough of it and we cannot market it.
% of butchering , keeping and selling? We sell 70% of males, keep all females.
Pre-tractor Days - You farmed the same crops...You wished for rain. How did you guarantee yourself against bad times? Drought? We did not prepare ourselves for bad crops. That is why we farm different crops.  
IMPORTANCE OF CROP ALTERNATION against bad years...
How much does each HH have? It varies; some 50, some 300 d. I have 150 d. land: 75 d: wheat, barley, rye; 15 d:chickpea; 30 d. sunflower; 15 d. lentil; rest is melon. This way we try to balance...We sell chickpea and lentils and melons..above our needs.
This land is very rocky, mountainous...mountains limit our  boundaries. Therefore, we cannot expand. Water is continuous, comes from mountain springs. It is for drinking only, both for men and animals. Herds stay on high land during summer; goats stay all season because we don’t milk them. Milking sheep are brought every evening for milking, then go back to mountain pens.

We have Kusburnu, good ahlat..
What kind of wheat? Kunduru which is used for good bread flour. Now they use it for macaroni. We don’t plant it anymore, it is not too productive. We plant Rus Bugdayı. Is there any well irrigation here? No, experts say it is 100s of m. below..so we can’t use it. No onion, no sugar beet.

This is however, very productive land, even without water. There is coolness here, this soil is very productive. It is even better than that of SÖ.
Productivity: 1/10 teneke (1 teneke seed yields 10 teneke wheat) to  1/35, without irrigation.
Of course, we work harder to prepare the land...Our tractor parts wear down in a shorter time than that of SÖ.. In one day, they do 50 d., we do 20 d.
In the past, we cleared the stones, now we clear them again...Harvesting is also very difficult. 

Married man live with his parents, in the same courtyard. Of her 8 kids, only 1 son lives here. 
Do you have ovens? Yes, we had it..because my wife was from Çekirdeksiz, Bulgarian emigre..so she built one in the courtyard...because they make somun, you know. 
Archaeological implication: So, she introduced that oven to this village.

